RT-07 follow-up (MG10 1f): set the zero-width line bias
(build-tree patch against isoriano1968/x11r6.3-amix cb61a21, applied after
rt07b-small-fills.patch; built as s12rt04).

The rtg/ screen is not built on miScreenInit, the only caller of
miSetZeroLineBias, so miZeroLineScreenIndex stayed 0 and miZeroLine took its
Bresenham tie-breaks from devPrivates[0] -- a heap pointer.  Diagonal edges
drawn with zero-width lines (olwm's window-menu triangle) therefore changed
with every build that moved the heap: s8rt04 and s10rt04 differ by 7 pixels
there with no drawing code changed, and the zline client's checksum differs
for s8/s10/s11.  rtgScreenInit now sets DEFAULTZEROLINEBIAS, the value every
miScreenInit server uses.  Rig: s12rt04 and a heap-shifted s12 (a 64 KiB
data array added) give the same zline checksum and the same triangle.

Antti's R5 Xrtg: R5's mi has no zero-line bias, so nothing to port there.

--- a/programs/Xserver/hw/amix/rtg/rtgInit.c
+++ b/programs/Xserver/hw/amix/rtg/rtgInit.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include "servermd.h"
 #include <scrnintstr.h>
 #include "mipointer.h"
+#include "miline.h"
 #include "mi.h"
 #include "rtg.h"
 #include "opaque.h"		/* dispatchException, DE_TERMINATE */
@@ -285,6 +286,15 @@
 
     pScreen->devPrivates[rtgScreenIndex].ptr = (pointer) pRTG;
 
+    /*
+    ** The zero-width line bias, which miScreenInit sets and this screen
+    ** (not built on miScreenInit) never did: without it miZeroLineScreenIndex
+    ** stays 0 and miZeroLine takes its Bresenham tie-breaks from whatever
+    ** devPrivates[0] holds -- a heap pointer -- so diagonal edges changed
+    ** with every build (MG10: the window-menu triangle, s8rt04 vs s10rt04).
+    */
+    miSetZeroLineBias(pScreen, DEFAULTZEROLINEBIAS);
+
     /* Screen geometry from selected mode */
     pRTG->width        = ZZ9000_selected_mode->w;
     pRTG->height       = ZZ9000_selected_mode->h;
